Allergic Rhinitis
Common Infections and Afflictions
Paroxysmal sneezing, Clear watery rhinorrhea, Pale boggy turbinates

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: CIA-008
Reason for Visit: Uncontrollable sneezing fits and acute severe nasal obstruction.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient experiencing acute, severe respiratory distress secondary to sudden, total nasal occlusion and relentless paroxysmal sneezing, leading to a sensation of choking and panic. The explosive nature of these upper airway symptoms necessitates an immediate physical assessment to evaluate airway patency, definitively exclude evolving anaphylaxis (laryngeal edema), and aggressively establish a clear airway. Delaying this assessment and intervention until the next in-hours period poses an unacceptable risk of escalating respiratory compromise and severe patient distress, fulfilling criteria for MBS urgent after-hours item numbers 585-600.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: I can't stop sneezing, my nose is completely blocked solid, and I feel like I'm suffocating when I try to breathe.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms initiated 12 hours ago with sudden, paroxysmal sneezing fits following exposure to a dusty environment during home renovations. This rapidly escalated to profuse, clear, and watery rhinorrhea. Over the last 4 hours, severe nasal congestion has completely obstructed nasal breathing. Patient reports intense itching of the nose, palate, and throat, alongside bilateral ocular pruritus and tearing. A postnasal drip is causing a highly irritating, chronic dry cough that exacerbates the sensation of airway compromise. Denies wheezing, stridor, hives, or swelling of the lips/tongue.
Functional Status: Independent, but completely functionally derailed by the acute symptom burden and subsequent panic.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone. High distress due to the subjective feeling of asphyxiation from total nasal obstruction.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 130/84 mmHg | HR: 96 bpm | Temp: 36.7 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Alert, mouth-breathing heavily. Voice is distinctly hyponasal. Visible dark circles under the eyes (infraorbital darkening). Frequent sneezing and throat clearing observed.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available.
Physical Exam: Anterior rhinoscopy reveals entirely pale, bluish, and severely boggy nasal turbinates bilaterally, causing total physical occlusion of the nasal meatus. Copious clear, watery secretions are present. A transverse nasal crease is faintly visible across the lower third of the nose. Oropharynx demonstrates cobblestoning of the posterior pharyngeal wall due to postnasal drip. Lungs are definitively clear to auscultation with equal air entry bilaterally; no wheeze or prolonged expiratory phase.
Environmental Observations: Visible dust in the hallway from ongoing renovations.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Patient has not attempted any over-the-counter antihistamines.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Allergic Rhinitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Acute Viral Rhinopharyngitis (Common Cold) - Ruled out due to the extreme suddenness of onset, the intense localized pruritus of the palate/eyes, the specific pale/bluish appearance of the turbinates (rather than erythematous), and the lack of viral prodrome (malaise, low-grade fever).
Anaphylaxis - Definitively ruled out due to clear lung fields without bronchospasm, normal hemodynamics, absence of generalized urticaria, and a fully patent lower/laryngeal airway despite severe upper nasal obstruction.
Nasal Polyposis - Unlikely to present with such a sudden, explosive acute crisis without a long, chronic history of progressive obstruction, though underlying minor polyps cannot be fully excluded until acute edema subsides.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The explosive onset of total nasal obstruction and uncontrollable sneezing created acute respiratory panic in the patient, mimicking early signs of a severe systemic hypersensitivity reaction. Urgent home assessment was essential to perform a rapid airway evaluation, definitively rule out impending laryngeal edema or bronchospasm, and immediately decompress the upper airway. Clinical examination confirmed a severe, isolated localized allergic response (acute allergic rhinitis). Immediate administration of potent systemic antihistamines and topical decongestants definitively broke the allergic cycle and restored nasal patency. This necessary, rapid clinical intervention could not be delayed until regular consulting hours, satisfying MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Administered Fexofenadine 180mg orally stat. Prescribed a short course of topical Oxymetazoline 0.05% nasal spray (maximum 3 days) to immediately shrink boggy turbinates. Initiated Mometasone 50mcg nasal spray, 2 sprays per nostril daily, as maintenance therapy to suppress the underlying mucosal inflammation.
Positive Behavioral Change: To reduce the ongoing allergic trigger, isolate the renovation area by taping plastic sheeting over the doors. Taking the oral antihistamine first thing in the morning creates a pharmacological blockade before dust exposure peaks during the day, proactively preventing the swelling before it starts.
Home-Based Services: None required.
DME/Safety: High-efficiency particulate air (HEPA) filter recommended for the bedroom.
Follow-up: Routine review with regular General Practitioner in 7-14 days to assess the efficacy of the maintenance steroid nasal spray.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Call 000 immediately if experiencing any difficulty swallowing, a sensation of the throat closing, swelling of the lips or tongue, or audible wheezing.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is confirmed clinically; identification of specific aeroallergens may require outpatient skin prick or serum IgE testing if symptoms become chronic.
Clinical Course: The acute sneezing and watery discharge will resolve within hours of antihistamine use. The severe nasal congestion will improve rapidly with the decongestant spray, but long-term control requires consistent, daily use of the steroid spray.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate medical attention if you develop a severe, productive cough, high fevers, or localized severe facial pain.

PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: A massive allergic reaction has caused the tissues inside your nose to swell like water balloons. The immediate decongestant spray pops the balloons for quick relief, while the daily steroid spray acts as a fire blanket, slowly putting out the underlying allergic inflammation so the swelling doesn't return.
Actionable Steps: Use the decongestant spray for no more than 3 days to avoid rebound congestion. When using the steroid spray, look down at your toes and aim the nozzle toward the outer ear; this targets the medication to the inflamed turbinates and prevents it from simply running down the back of your throat.

SMS INFORMATION
